                <pb facs="0627" n="1281" sameAs="frus:frus1875v02/pg1281"/>

                <head>No. 619.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Upton to Mr. Fish.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Berne</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1874-12-18"><hi rendition="#italic">December</hi> 18, 1874</date>.
                        (Received January 9, 1875.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 8.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to state as follows;
                    Yesterday the two houses of the federal assembly met in convention to elect a
                    President and Vice-President of the Swiss Confederation for the ensuing year.
                    Mr. Scherer, of Winterthur, was chosen President on the first ballot, having 102
                    out of 154 votes. Mr. Borel, of Neufchatel, was in like manner elected
                    Vice-President, receiving 101 out of 150 votes. Mr. Welti was, according to
                    custom, to have been the President, but it was desired that he should keep his
                    present war department until the army is organized under the new
                    constitution.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>CHAS. H. UPTON.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
